Patch: compile the pinned SR translator for WASI
Upstream: https://github.com/M-HT/SR at ac690ddf3010bc3d2c875cb1b5e8da4f53a8d5b3, after sr-x64.patch.
Original: the loader includes nonstandard malloc.h and main lowers the host process priority with nice().
Replacement: include standard stdlib.h and skip priority adjustment on WASI.
Effect: the same x64 translator runs inside a browser or a sandboxed local WASI runtime. Native priority adjustment is unchanged.
Why: WASI has no process priority service and its standard allocator declarations live in stdlib.h. The build links WASI's mmap emulation so SR's existing buffered-read fallback remains available; C++ containers are built with exceptions disabled because allocation failure aborts the isolated build.
Evidence: the supported reference RGFX.EXE produces byte-identical rgfx.asm and all seven postprocessed engine sections with the native and WASI translators. No game bytes are included in this patch.

--- a/SR/SR_loader.c
+++ b/SR/SR_loader.c
@@ -23,7 +23,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <stdio.h>
-#include <malloc.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
 #include <string.h>
 #include "SR_defs.h"
 #include "SR_vars.h"
--- a/SR/SR_main.c
+++ b/SR/SR_main.c
@@ -265,7 +265,7 @@ int main (int argc, char *argv[])
 
 #ifdef _WIN32
 	SetPriorityClass(GetCurrentProcess(), BELOW_NORMAL_PRIORITY_CLASS);
-#else
+#elif !defined(__wasi__)
 	if (-1 == nice(1)) {}
 #endif
 
